// Test: #|c| gives a character's code point, an Int (D-25, v0.0.12).
// `#|…|` is the door to the number, and a character is a number: its code.
// It was `##!c` from v0.0.8 to v0.0.11, which no longer takes a Char, and
// `0d|c|` for a day of v0.0.12 (D-24), which is text again.

>> #|'A'| ¶        // 65
>> #|'z'| ¶        // 122
>> #|'0'| ¶        // 48
>> #|' '| ¶        // 32
>> #|'あ'| ¶       // 12354  (hiragana A)
>> #|'⚫'| ¶       // 9899

// Result type is Int
r = #|'A'|
meta = r#?
>> meta[1] ¶       // ###

// Iterating a string yields Chars; #|…| turns each into its code point
total = 0
@ c:"ABC" {
    total = total + #|c|
}
>> total ¶         // 65+66+67 = 198

// Usable in a range classification (is 'M' an ASCII uppercase letter?)
p = #|'M'|
? p >= 65 && p <= 90 {
    >> "upper" ¶
}

// A base literal is a Char, so its code is #|…| of it
>> #|0x41| " " #|0b11111111| " " #|0o101| ¶   // 65 255 65

// The digit's value is the text's reading, not the character's
siete = '7'
>> #|siete| " " #|"{siete}"| ¶   // 55 7
